<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mogao Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caves of the Thousand Buddhas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mogao Caves are a complex of nearly 500 Buddhist cave temples cut into a cliff south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from the 4th to the 14th century. They hold some of the finest Buddhist murals and sculptures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. In 1900 a sealed library cave was found there containing tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the Dunhuang manuscripts, and the site became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogao-caves.jpg" alt="Mogao Caves" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mogao_Caves_(54376969262).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
